## The three capstone checks

### 1. VOICE SPEC block present — PASS

Both narrators resolve through #247's rule (c) and both system prompts carry the block.
The old render could not have had it: its files need rule (c), which landed five days after
it ran.

### 2. Vukradin's sardonic-operator beats soften toward the spec — PASS

`voice/vukradin_new_pipeline.md` line 58 states the rule the old render broke:

> Do not make Vukradin sound cynical, smirking, or sardonic. He is sincere. Snark is not
> his connective tissue.

Same beat, both renders — Vukradin hands over the statue and apologises:

**OLD** (operator register; self-aggrandising, past tense):

> I had carried this statue across half the Sword Coast. I had refused a dragon's hoard for
> less principle than this. I walked up with it in both hands.

**NEW** (sincere register; the apology is examined, not scored):

> *Sorry.* The word lands wrong even as I say it — an apology stapled to an introduction —
> but there is no graceful place in any sentence to put a death.

This is a judgement, not a measurement. But it is the exact softening the capstone
predicted, on the exact narrator it named, with the spec as the changed input.

### 3. Present tense throughout, hatch still firing — PASS, and this is the headline

The genre's line 12 says: **"First-person present tense, always"**. The old render ignored
it. The new one obeys it.

| Scene | OLD past:present | NEW past:present |
|---|---|---|
| 02 (Brewbarry) | 65:9 — past | 8:49 — present |
| 04 (Vukradin) | 31:4 — past | 7:30 — present |

Scene 04 went from 31 past-tense verbs against 4 present, to 7 against 30. **The tense rule
was in the rulebook the whole time and was not reaching the model.** That is what a 16K
one-line `GENRE:` label costs, and it is the strongest evidence so far for #276 fix 1.

The table-speech hatch fired once in each new scene and zero times in either old scene.

## New finding — the critic's em-dash scan contradicts the genre

Em-dashes in prose rose in scene 04: 7 -> 17 (14.8 -> 21.7 per 1000 prose words).

This is not obviously a defect. `voice/_genre.md` line 39 **sanctions** the device:

> Italics for one-word thoughts; em-dash for interrupted speech or thought.

The new sincere-register interiority beats are exactly "interrupted thought", so the rise
is the genre being followed. But `/voice-critic`'s mechanical scan A flags *every*
narration-level em-dash for conversion. So the checker and the rulebook disagree, and the
checker will now report ~17 findings per Vukradin scene that the genre permits.

That is the hand-copied-fork problem `VoiceCriticAlignment_proposal.md` describes, caught
in the act. It belongs in kostadis/mytools#125 as a concrete case for D2 (read the
effective rulebook) — and it strengthens the argument, because scan A is not merely stale,
it is wrong against a rule the genre states plainly.

## What this does not establish

- Two variables changed together (voice specs, genre delivery). The tense result is
  attributable to the genre block; the register result is attributable to the spec; neither
  attribution is isolated by a third run.
- One session, two scenes, two narrators. Not a corpus.
- No `/voice-critic` re-score was run. It would now be measured against a rulebook fork
  that disagrees with the genre on em-dashes, so fixing #125's scan A first would make the
  score mean more.
- The old files remain the campaign's narration. Nothing here was promoted.

